getpeername on a PF_LOCAL socket smashed the caller stack.

sockaddr_fixup_from_linux converts a Linux sockaddr into a BSD one in place, in
the caller's buffer, and it was told how many bytes the kernel WROTE but never
how large that buffer IS. For a PF_LOCAL address it then wrote a full
sizeof(sun_path) plus a terminator, which is 106 bytes.

bash calls it for every shell whose stdin is a socket. lib/sh/netconn.c does

	socklen_t l; struct sockaddr sa;
	l = sizeof(sa); rv = getpeername(fd, &sa, &l);

with a 16 byte struct sockaddr on the stack, so 106 bytes landed over it and
bash died in __stack_chk_fail, silently, with SIGABRT. Under the NixOS test
driver, whose control channel is a socketpair, that read as Darling hanging.

Measured: AF_UNIX stdin aborts, AF_INET stdin does not, which is the PF_LOCAL
branch exactly. The stack from strace -k is
isnetconn -> __stack_chk_fail -> abort -> kill(0, SIGABRT).

The same helper is reached by getsockname and recvfrom, and by the three route
message writers in sysctl_net, whose slots are one
round_up_sizeof_u32(sizeof(struct sockaddr)) each. All of them now pass the
capacity, and every write is bounded by it. The return value stays the FULL
length so a caller still learns the true size when the address did not fit,
which is how truncation works on macOS.

Two smaller things fixed with it: the path was read out of sun_path with strcpy
regardless of how much the kernel wrote, so an unnamed socket was read as an
uninitialised string; and an unnamed socket now returns without a pointless
vchroot round trip.

diff --git a/cider/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/include/xnu_syscall/bsd/helper/network/duct.h b/cider/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/include/xnu_syscall/bsd/helper/network/duct.h
--- a/cider/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/include/xnu_syscall/bsd/helper/network/duct.h
+++ b/cider/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/include/xnu_syscall/bsd/helper/network/duct.h
@@ -20,6 +20,9 @@
 
 unsigned long sockaddr_fixup_size_from_bsd(const void* bsd_sockaddr, int bsd_sockaddr_len);
 int sockaddr_fixup_from_bsd(struct sockaddr_fixup* out, const void* bsd_sockaddr, int bsd_sockaddr_len);
-int sockaddr_fixup_from_linux(struct sockaddr_fixup* out, const void* linux_sockaddr, int linux_sockaddr_len);
+// `capacity` is the size of the CALLER's buffer, which is not the same thing as the
+// number of bytes the Linux kernel wrote into it. Without it the PF_LOCAL conversion
+// wrote a full 104 byte sun_path into whatever it was given.
+int sockaddr_fixup_from_linux(struct sockaddr_fixup* out, const void* linux_sockaddr, int linux_sockaddr_len, int capacity);
 
 #endif // NETWORK_DUCT_H
